1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,502
Chai Corner has six people now.

2
00:00:02,802 --> 00:00:04,136
Ravi pays them well.

3
00:00:04,136 --> 00:00:06,957
But the team is quiet,
and the chai is slow.

4
00:00:07,507 --> 00:00:09,153
He offers more money.

5
00:00:09,153 --> 00:00:10,403
Nothing changes.

6
00:00:10,703 --> 00:00:13,753
Then one evening, he sits
down with them, and listens.

7
00:00:14,053 --> 00:00:16,271
Next morning, the team works faster.

8
00:00:16,921 --> 00:00:18,616
Money was not missing.

9
00:00:18,616 --> 00:00:19,756
Attention was.

10
00:00:20,456 --> 00:00:25,343
Meet Elton Mayo, an Australian-born
professor at Harvard Business School.

11
00:00:25,643 --> 00:00:29,960
With a small Harvard team, he studied
what really makes people work hard.

12
00:00:30,310 --> 00:00:34,108
Their ideas became known as
the human relations approach.

13
00:00:34,758 --> 00:00:37,475
So, what is the human relations approach?

14
00:00:37,775 --> 00:00:41,584
It says people work better when they
feel noticed, valued, and part of

15
00:00:41,584 --> 00:00:42,484
a team.

16
00:00:42,539 --> 00:00:45,142
Taylor saw workers as parts of a machine.

17
00:00:45,142 --> 00:00:46,781
Mayo saw them as people.

18
00:00:47,431 --> 00:00:52,691
It began at the Hawthorne Works, a huge
Western Electric factory near Chicago.

19
00:00:53,041 --> 00:00:59,684
From 1924 to 1932, researchers
ran tests there in four stages.

20
00:01:00,284 --> 00:01:01,245
Stage one.

21
00:01:01,245 --> 00:01:02,770
The lighting tests.

22
00:01:03,120 --> 00:01:06,293
Lights went up in one room,
and stayed the same in another.

23
00:01:06,643 --> 00:01:08,636
Output rose in both rooms.

24
00:01:08,636 --> 00:01:11,081
So light was not the real cause.

25
00:01:11,681 --> 00:01:12,683
Stage two.

26
00:01:12,683 --> 00:01:16,588
Six women assembled telephone
relays in a test room.

27
00:01:16,938 --> 00:01:20,974
Researchers changed their breaks and
hours, and talked with them often.

28
00:01:21,324 --> 00:01:23,919
Output went up through
most of the changes.

29
00:01:23,919 --> 00:01:26,605
They felt noticed, and
became a close team.

30
00:01:27,205 --> 00:01:28,206
Stage three.

31
00:01:28,206 --> 00:01:32,321
About twenty thousand workers
were interviewed about their jobs.

32
00:01:32,671 --> 00:01:35,754
Mayo saw that simply being
listened to mattered to them.

33
00:01:36,354 --> 00:01:37,372
Stage four.

34
00:01:37,372 --> 00:01:40,943
Fourteen men wired telephone
equipment as a group.

35
00:01:41,293 --> 00:01:43,611
They made their own unwritten rules.

36
00:01:43,611 --> 00:01:45,891
Don't work too fast, or too slow.

37
00:01:46,241 --> 00:01:48,708
The group set the pace, not the pay plan.

38
00:01:49,358 --> 00:01:50,958
So, what did Mayo learn?

39
00:01:51,308 --> 00:01:52,498
People are social.

40
00:01:52,498 --> 00:01:53,832
They want to belong.

41
00:01:54,182 --> 00:01:55,693
Attention lifts output.

42
00:01:55,693 --> 00:01:57,998
This is called the Hawthorne effect.

43
00:01:58,348 --> 00:02:01,126
Informal groups shape
how hard people work.

44
00:02:01,476 --> 00:02:04,800
And money matters, but it
is not the only motivator.

45
00:02:05,450 --> 00:02:07,927
So what does this look like at work today?

46
00:02:08,277 --> 00:02:09,208
Recognition.

47
00:02:09,208 --> 00:02:10,782
Thank people by name.

48
00:02:10,782 --> 00:02:13,828
Many companies have an
employee of the month.

49
00:02:14,178 --> 00:02:15,078
Listening.

50
00:02:14,985 --> 00:02:16,778
Ask people how they feel.

51
00:02:16,778 --> 00:02:19,745
Offices run staff
surveys, and act on them.

52
00:02:20,095 --> 00:02:20,995
Belonging.

53
00:02:20,933 --> 00:02:22,256
Build real teams.

54
00:02:22,256 --> 00:02:24,819
New joiners get a buddy on day one.

55
00:02:25,169 --> 00:02:26,296
Informal groups.

56
00:02:26,296 --> 00:02:28,570
Work with them, not against them.

57
00:02:28,570 --> 00:02:31,788
A smart manager wins over
the team's natural leader.

58
00:02:32,138 --> 00:02:33,118
Beyond money.

59
00:02:33,118 --> 00:02:34,992
Care about the whole person.

60
00:02:34,992 --> 00:02:37,548
Flexible hours help parents and students.

61
00:02:38,198 --> 00:02:39,519
So, was Mayo right?

62
00:02:39,519 --> 00:02:40,419
Partly.

63
00:02:40,435 --> 00:02:41,479
The good side.

64
00:02:41,479 --> 00:02:45,542
He put people back into management:
teams, feelings, and talking.

65
00:02:45,892 --> 00:02:46,955
The hard side.

66
00:02:46,955 --> 00:02:50,229
The studies were small, and
not carefully controlled.

67
00:02:50,579 --> 00:02:54,021
In 2011, economists found
the old lighting data.

68
00:02:54,021 --> 00:02:57,144
The effect was much
smaller than the legend.

69
00:02:57,494 --> 00:03:00,529
And happy workers are not
always more productive.

70
00:03:00,529 --> 00:03:02,981
Fair pay and clear goals still matter.

71
00:03:03,331 --> 00:03:07,145
So the lesson is: care about
people, and keep measuring the work.

72
00:03:07,795 --> 00:03:08,695
Quick check.

73
00:03:08,676 --> 00:03:12,454
Ravi starts paying attention to
his team, and they work harder.

74
00:03:12,454 --> 00:03:13,808
What is this called?

75
00:03:15,708 --> 00:03:17,033
The Hawthorne effect!

76
00:03:17,033 --> 00:03:19,514
People work better when they feel noticed.

77
00:03:20,114 --> 00:03:21,123
So, remember.

78
00:03:21,423 --> 00:03:27,711
The Hawthorne studies ran from
1924 to 1932, near Chicago.

79
00:03:28,061 --> 00:03:31,718
They showed that workers are
social beings, not machines.

80
00:03:32,068 --> 00:03:36,173
Attention, belonging and good
teams raise output, not just money.

81
00:03:36,773 --> 00:03:41,522
Next time, we'll see a business as one
big system, in the systems approach.
